    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders receive 4 sets and 10.4% of volume, which is lower than chest and back. Given that the strength index for shoulders is also flat, some additional direct shoulder work could help balance the upper-body push pattern."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs receive 6 sets but only 22.4% of volume share, which is the lowest of the three major compound groups. For a muscle-building goal, legs typically benefit from being at least on par with or exceeding upper-body groups in set count."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Increase the load or add one working set on at least one shoulder exercise next period — the flat strength index (103 to 103) signals your current shoulder stimulus is not driving adaptation.",
    "Add a fourth set to your biceps work, bringing it from 3 to 4 sets. At only 4% of total volume, biceps are the most undertrained group relative to your muscle-building goal.",
    "On your lower-body sessions, push for a small but deliberate weight increase — legs rose 2 points this period, which is encouraging, and maintaining that upward trend with progressive load increases will help you cross the +3 threshold.",
    "For chest and back, which showed the most movement this period, maintain your current set count but focus on completing every rep with good technique before adding weight, to ensure the strength index gains continue next period."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Stalling period with narrow strength index gains on chest, back, and legs (+1 to +2 points each) and no movement on shoulders, biceps, or triceps; action plan focuses on increasing biceps volume and shoulder intensity to break the plateau."
}
